---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';

/**
 * The standing answer to "what am I looking at", which the nav's parenthetical
 * points at.
 *
 * It is a page and not only a blog post because it is the site's account of
 * itself, and a dated entry in a feed is the wrong shape for that: a reader who
 * arrives in a year should not be reading something filed under a month. The
 * long first-person version is the post; this states the claim and stops.
 */
const POST = '/blog/the-most-underrated-sefer-i-own';
---

<Layout
  title="What this is — Derech Tevunos"
  description="A site that draws a sugya as a picture of its own argument, using Ramchal's Derech Tevunos as the labelling system. Why it exists, and why the labelling only became practical now."
>
  <div class="dt-page">
    <article class="wrap">
      <header class="head">
        <div class="head-copy">
          <h1 class="dt-page-title">What this is</h1>
          <p class="dt-page-description">
            A drawing of a sugya's argument, and the 1742 sefer that says what to draw.
          </p>
        </div>
        {/* The standing pose: gyro in hand. Decorative, so `alt` is empty —
            the heading beside it already says what the page is, and a
            description of the drawing would only add noise to a screen reader. */}
        <img
          class="pose"
          src="/mascot/pose.webp"
          alt=""
          width="427"
          height="620"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </header>

      <div class="dt-prose">
        <div class="callout">
          <p class="callout-label">In short</p>
          <p>
            Ramchal's <em>Derech Tevunos</em> gives every sentence of a sugya a
            label: what the sentence <strong>does</strong> to the one before it,
            what it <strong>is</strong> once you state it plainly, and how it is
            <strong>warranted</strong>. This site labels passages that way and
            draws the result, so the shape of an argument is something you can
            look at instead of hold in your head.
          </p>
          <p>
            I built it because the labelling is enormous, patient, mechanical
            work, and that is the first kind of work machines got good at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>The sefer</h2>
        <p>
          <em>Derech Tevunos</em>, <em>The Ways of Reason</em>, is a manual for
          reading how a sugya moves. Chapter 9 gives the moves a closed
          vocabulary: 7 elements and 19 leaves, each one a thing a sentence does
          to an earlier sentence, each with a test that separates it from the
          label sitting next to it. Chapter 3 reduces any utterance, however it
          was phrased, to a subject and a predicate in one of 11 manners. Chapter
          4 says how two statements can stand to each other, and when an apparent
          dispute is not one. Chapters 7 and 8 say what a proof has to accomplish
          to land, what a rebuttal does to the claim it hits, and where a claim
          sits when nothing has been established about it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Closed vocabularies, a procedure, and a discriminating test for each
          label against its nearest neighbour. That is a specification, written
          284 years before anything could execute it.
        </p>

        <h2>Why it went unused</h2>
        <p>
          The work is enormous. In chapter 10 Ramchal takes four short Aramaic
          sentences from <em>Shabbos</em> 5b and reconstructs what is underneath
          them: two claims, four syllogisms and an elucidation, roughly a dozen
          steps, none of them written in the Gemara and every one of them
          required. It is the most impressive passage in the sefer and it is also
          the bill. Four sentences took him pages. Nobody has the hours to do
          that to a daf, let alone a masechta.
        </p>

        <h2>What changed</h2>
        <p>
          The labelling stopped being expensive. The question a model answers
          here is narrow: what does this sentence do to the sentence before it?
          The answer has to be one of 19 labels, it has to name the sentence it
          acts on, and it has to survive Ramchal's own test for telling that
          label from its nearest neighbour. An answer outside the vocabulary
          fails validation and the file never draws.
        </p>
        <p>
          A model still gets things wrong, so every passage here was checked by
          hand. The drawing is what makes checking fast: a sentence labelled
          wrong takes the picture with it, and you catch it before you have
          finished reading the row.
        </p>

        <h2>Why a drawing</h2>
        <p>
          Two reasons, and the second one is the honest one. A picture is the
          quickest way to check a labelling. And a great many more people will
          look at a well-made drawing than will ever read an argument about
          dialectical method. If this sefer gets used, it will be because
          somebody saw the shape of an argument, found it beautiful, and got
          curious about what produced it.
        </p>

        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/sugya">passages</a> are worked examples, 5 of Ramchal's
          own and 4 opening sugyot. The <a href="/docs">sefer itself</a> is here
          in Hebrew and English, all 11 chapters, with
          <a href="/docs/text/chapter-09">chapter 9</a> as the vocabulary the
          drawing reads. A file you label yourself gets
          <a href="/byo">drawn by the same code</a> the shipped passages get.
        </p>
        <p>
          The longer version of the argument on this page is
          <a href={POST}>the most underrated sefer I own</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 2rem 4rem;
  }

  .head {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 2rem;
  }

  .head-copy {
    max-width: 62ch;
    /* The gradient bar under the title is a ::after pinned to the element's
       edges, so the heading must not stretch to the flex line's full width or
       the rule runs past the words. */
    flex: 0 1 auto;
  }

  /* Same 132px as the docs index figure, and for the same reason: at 168px
     the flex row outgrew the title-and-deck and left a hole above the heading.
     Negative margin drops the boots onto the deck's baseline. */
  .pose {
    width: 132px;
    height: auto;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    align-self: flex-end;
    margin-bottom: -1.25rem;
  }

  /* The about column is 760px, so the figure still has room at widths where
     the 1100px docs index already hides its own. Hide only on a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .pose { display: none; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .wrap { padding: 1.5rem 1rem 3rem; }
  }
</style>
